#{[17592186345003 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y}=\\frac{{1}^{2}-1}{\\sqrt{1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{({y}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{(\\sqrt{x}-y)(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{({y}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{-(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{x-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}-(\\sqrt{x}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}-(\\sqrt{x}+y) & =-(\\sqrt{1}+1) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"] [17592186345005 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=$"] [17592186345005 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0\\cdot {0}^{3}}{{0}^{6}+8\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{6}+8{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}({x}^{4}+8)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+8} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{3}}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}+8{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{9{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186363774 :question/problem "A sealed medical courier capsule of mass $9\\,\\text{kg}$ is released from rest with an initial downward velocity of $4.6\\,\\text{m/s}.$ Let $v(t)$ be the capsule's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction. The air resistance acting on the capsule is negligible, and the only other force acting on it is its weight due to gravity.\n\nFind the equation for $v(t).$ Express your answer in terms of time $t$ and acceleration due to gravity $g.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186363804 :question/problem "A research module of mass $20\\,\\text{kg}$ is released from rest and falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the module's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the module is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the module’s velocity is $10\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the module are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t),$ expressing your answer in terms of $t$ **only.**\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186363807 :question/problem "A communications beacon of mass $24\\,\\text{kg}$ is launched downward with an initial velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s}$ and continues to fall vertically. Let $v(t)$ be the beacon's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after launch, with downward taken as the positive direction.\n\nThe air resistance on the beacon is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the beacon’s velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the beacon are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t),$ expressing your answer in terms of $t$ **only.**\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186363810 :question/problem "A sampling canister of mass $18\\,\\text{kg}$ is launched downward with an initial velocity of $3\\,\\text{m/s}$ and continues to fall vertically. Let $v(t)$ be the canister's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after launch, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the canister is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the canister’s velocity is $9\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $6.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the canister are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t),$ expressing your answer in terms of $t$ **only.**\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186377695 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}.$"] [17592186377695 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}=\\frac{1\\cdot (-3)-(-3)}{1-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{(x-1)y}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-3.$"] [17592186377708 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=$"] [17592186377708 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}\\cdot 0}{{0}^{3}+{0}^{3}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths. \n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=2x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+8{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186378622 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (2,\\pi )}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$"] [17592186378622 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(2,\\pi )$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,\\pi )}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =2(-1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186378629 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy}.$"] [17592186378629 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}-0(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{x(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{1}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{1}{x-y} & =\\frac{1}{0-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $1.$"] [17592186378636 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$"] [17592186378636 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{0}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186380041 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,-1)}\\sqrt{x-y+z}=\n$$"] [17592186380041 :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x-y+z}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the expression under the radical is negative. However, the point $(3,1,-1)$ does not cause the  expression under the radical  to be negative, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-3,1,-1)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,-1)}\\sqrt{x-y+z} & =\\sqrt{3-1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3-1-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186380048 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-2)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz}=$"] [17592186380048 :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(1,1,-2)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,1,-2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-2)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz} & =\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+{1}^{2}\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot (-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-2}{1+1-2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186380055 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x}.$"] [17592186380055 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x}=\\frac{{0}^{2}-0\\cdot 2}{{0}^{3}-2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x} & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x(x-y)}{x({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}+2} & =\\frac{0-2}{0+2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-1.$"] [17592186380242 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}.$"] [17592186380242 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)+1}{(-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x(x+y)+(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+1)(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$"]}
